# Operator-declared protected paths.
#
# See governance/GATE_MANIFEST_INTEGRITY.md. This file DECLARES the paths whose authoritative
# owner is the operator -- it does not by itself enforce that. CODEOWNERS only has teeth when
# branch protection on `main` has "Require review from Code Owners" enabled.
#
# core/RISK_REGISTER.md R12/R13 (measured 2026-09-11, still current): the live ruleset on `main`
# has `require_code_owner_review: false` and `required_approving_review_count: 0` -- deliberately,
# because a single GitHub identity plus GitHub's own self-approval ban would deadlock every PR
# otherwise. So this file is currently DECORATION in the sense that no platform control reads it to
# block a merge: separation between implementer and operator is procedural (the implementer's own
# compliance plus the audit trail in core/DECISION_LOG.md), not mechanical. No document in this
# repository may claim otherwise -- R13's own binding resolution. This file still has real value:
# it is the authoritative RECORD of which paths are operator-owned, read by
# tests/policy/codeowners.test.mjs, and it would start mechanically enforcing the moment
# code-owner review is turned back on. (Global CLAUDE.md §24's authority-surface carve-out, which
# used to keep a PR touching these paths on an operator-only merge track regardless of GPT-PM
# review, was removed globally on 2026-09-12 -- see ~/.claude/core/DECISION_LOG.md D-005. A PR
# touching these paths now merges under §24's ordinary conditions like any other PR.)

# Governance state: manifests and approvals define what a gate may do.
/governance/gate-manifests/     @xLZDx
/governance/operator-approvals/ @xLZDx

# CI cannot police its own definition: for `pull_request` events GitHub runs the workflow as it
# exists in the PR's merge commit, so a PR that edits these files edits the checks that police it.
/.github/                       @xLZDx

# The workflow above is a thin wrapper; the checks it runs live here. Leaving these unprotected
# while protecting `.github/` would mean the definition of a check is operator-owned but its
# implementation is not.
/scripts/verify/                @xLZDx

# Policy-sensitive files (TDD 57(13)).
/core/SOURCE_POLICY.md          @xLZDx
/core/DATA_RETENTION_POLICY.md  @xLZDx
/core/adr/                      @xLZDx

# The architecture baseline and everything that can override it.
#
# `TDD_ERRATA.md` is NORMATIVE and outranks `TDD.md` on concrete details, so an implementer able to
# edit it could change the binding architecture -- by declaring a restriction superseded, or by
# quoting an authority that was never given -- without touching any other operator-owned path.
# GPT-PM caught exactly that: the G1-M2 change created a new authority surface and left it outside
# the trust boundary the same change was tightening.
#
# `TDD.md` itself was never listed here either. That gap predates the errata file and is not
# something the errata introduced, but it is the same defect: the frozen baseline was editable
# without operator review. Both are covered by taking the directory.
/docs/architecture/             @xLZDx

# The operating contracts themselves.
/CLAUDE.md                      @xLZDx
/AGENTS.md                      @xLZDx
